1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,390
Everyone in this square says they're fine.

2
00:00:02,663 --> 00:00:05,133
In a second, you'll see the storms they're hiding.

3
00:00:05,531 --> 00:00:07,671
This morning, you woke up and saw weather.

4
00:00:07,691 --> 00:00:08,711
Not outside.

5
00:00:08,731 --> 00:00:09,921
Over people's heads.

6
00:00:10,233 --> 00:00:12,053
Sun means they're doing fine.

7
00:00:12,073 --> 00:00:14,453
Clouds, rain, storms mean they're not.

8
00:00:14,473 --> 00:00:15,573
And their faces?

9
00:00:15,593 --> 00:00:17,263
Their faces never change.

10
00:00:17,587 --> 00:00:18,607
Three rules.

11
00:00:18,627 --> 00:00:20,927
One: you can see everyone's inner weather.

12
00:00:20,947 --> 00:00:22,207
Two: they can't.

13
00:00:22,227 --> 00:00:24,607
Three: the only weather you can't see is

14
00:00:24,627 --> 00:00:25,227
your own.

15
00:00:26,022 --> 00:00:26,882
[STRANGER] Morning!

16
00:00:26,902 --> 00:00:28,332
Doing great!

17
00:00:28,485 --> 00:00:29,835
He was not doing great.

18
00:00:30,393 --> 00:00:32,463
Once you see it, you see it everywhere.

19
00:00:32,799 --> 00:00:33,509
[BEA] Morning!

20
00:00:33,599 --> 00:00:35,989
Fresh rolls, still warm!

21
00:00:36,061 --> 00:00:38,521
Bea, the baker, smiling behind her counter.

22
00:00:38,541 --> 00:00:39,801
A small cloud.

23
00:00:39,821 --> 00:00:42,051
Some days, that inner critic still talks.

24
00:00:42,434 --> 00:00:44,254
[FINN] What do you call a fish with no eyes?

25
00:00:44,274 --> 00:00:45,454
A fsh!

26
00:00:45,474 --> 00:00:48,904
Ha ha!

27
00:00:49,034 --> 00:00:51,334
Finn, the island's joker, cracking jokes

28
00:00:51,354 --> 00:00:52,054
on his boat.

29
00:00:52,074 --> 00:00:52,944
Drizzle.

30
00:00:53,274 --> 00:00:53,974
[KAI] Yep.

31
00:00:53,994 --> 00:00:54,624
All good.

32
00:00:54,783 --> 00:00:55,803
Kai, the builder.

33
00:00:55,823 --> 00:00:57,323
Calm, steady, always fine.

34
00:00:57,343 --> 00:00:58,133
A storm.

35
00:00:58,611 --> 00:01:00,111
And you'd thought you were the only one

36
00:01:00,131 --> 00:01:01,321
having a rough week.

37
00:01:01,877 --> 00:01:03,777
When researchers asked students to guess

38
00:01:03,797 --> 00:01:05,617
how many classmates had recently been

39
00:01:05,637 --> 00:01:08,497
through something hard, they guessed too low.

40
00:01:08,517 --> 00:01:10,817
And the lower the guess, the lonelier they

41
00:01:10,837 --> 00:01:11,707
tended to feel.

42
00:01:12,348 --> 00:01:13,698
So why don't we see it?

43
00:01:13,971 --> 00:01:15,791
Because we hide the rain and show the sunshine.

44
00:01:15,811 --> 00:01:18,591
In the same research, students kept about

45
00:01:18,611 --> 00:01:21,071
forty percent of their bad moments to themselves.

46
00:01:21,091 --> 00:01:22,671
Their good moments?

47
00:01:22,691 --> 00:01:24,761
Only thirteen percent.

48
00:01:25,308 --> 00:01:28,178
Most feeds are sunny, even when the sky isn't.

49
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:30,230
Then you see Ana.

50
00:01:30,755 --> 00:01:33,295
Ana, who said hello to a lonely stranger

51
00:01:33,315 --> 00:01:34,735
every day for a month.

52
00:01:34,755 --> 00:01:36,905
Ana, who shows up for everyone.

53
00:01:37,556 --> 00:01:39,626
Ana has the biggest storm on the island.

54
00:01:41,825 --> 00:01:43,415
So you ask how she's doing.

55
00:01:43,771 --> 00:01:44,481
[ANA] I'm fine!

56
00:01:45,030 --> 00:01:46,220
And then you ask again.

57
00:01:46,310 --> 00:01:47,010
No, really.

58
00:01:47,030 --> 00:01:47,740
How are you?

59
00:01:48,047 --> 00:01:48,987
[ANA] Honestly?

60
00:01:49,007 --> 00:01:52,277
I'm moving my mum's things on Saturday.

61
00:01:52,447 --> 00:01:53,867
By myself.

62
00:01:53,887 --> 00:01:55,957
I didn't want to bother anyone.

63
00:01:56,517 --> 00:01:57,617
You tell her to ask.

64
00:01:57,637 --> 00:01:58,507
Just ask.

65
00:01:59,019 --> 00:02:01,569
[ANA] Would anyone be able to help on Saturday?

66
00:02:02,373 --> 00:02:03,003
[THEO] Of course.

67
00:02:03,308 --> 00:02:05,058
[MO] I'll bring sandwiches!

68
00:02:05,224 --> 00:02:06,334
[KAI] I've got a van.

69
00:02:06,839 --> 00:02:09,059
In one experiment, people guessed they'd

70
00:02:09,079 --> 00:02:11,059
have to ask about twenty strangers to get

71
00:02:11,079 --> 00:02:13,059
five to do a small favor.

72
00:02:13,079 --> 00:02:14,509
They needed about ten.

73
00:02:14,877 --> 00:02:16,377
We're sure we'll be a burden.

74
00:02:16,397 --> 00:02:19,107
But people say yes far more often than we expect.

75
00:02:19,901 --> 00:02:21,881
And then Ana does something nobody's done

76
00:02:21,901 --> 00:02:22,611
all week.

77
00:02:22,963 --> 00:02:23,903
[ANA] What about you?

78
00:02:23,923 --> 00:02:25,193
How are you, really?

79
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:27,520
For the first time, you catch your

80
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:28,960
reflection in the window.

81
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:30,090
Your own weather.

82
00:02:30,488 --> 00:02:31,188
A cloud.

83
00:02:31,208 --> 00:02:32,068
Not the biggest.

84
00:02:32,088 --> 00:02:33,188
Not the smallest.

85
00:02:33,208 --> 00:02:34,718
Just weather, like everyone's.

86
00:02:35,499 --> 00:02:36,849
You were never the only one.

87
00:02:38,434 --> 00:02:40,254
You'll never see anyone's weather.

88
00:02:40,274 --> 00:02:42,334
But you can ask how someone's doing, and

89
00:02:42,354 --> 00:02:45,134
if they say fine, ask one more time.

90
00:02:45,154 --> 00:02:47,214
And if you're the one under the storm, let

91
00:02:47,234 --> 00:02:48,174
someone help.

92
00:02:48,194 --> 00:02:49,704
They'll probably say yes.

93
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:52,280
Everyone's carrying some weather.

94
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:54,130
You don't have to carry yours alone.
